  "question": {
    "text": "A glass bulb of the volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>400</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi>cm</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math> is connected to another bulb of volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>200</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi>cm</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math> by means of a tube of negligible volume. The bulbs contain dry air and are both at a common temperature and pressure of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>°</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>atm</mi></math>, respectively. The larger bulb is immersed in steam at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>°</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> and the smaller in melting ice at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>°</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>. Find the final common pressure."
  },
